    <h2>Google Imagen 3 vs Imagen 2 SynthID Coordinate Structure</h2>
    <p>
      Understanding the generational evolution between Google Imagen models is vital for configuring optimal sanitization thresholds. In Imagen 2, the watermark was primarily injected across intermediate spatial frequency bands corresponding to $8 	imes 8$ discrete cosine transform blocks. While resilient against standard JPEG compression, this earlier implementation exhibited mild vulnerabilities to high-pass spatial sharpening.
    </p>
    <p>
      With Imagen 3, Google DeepMind introduced multi-scale latent watermarking. Perturbations are embedded simultaneously across three distinct hierarchical resolution levels ($64 	imes 64$, $128 	imes 128$, and full canvas native resolution). Furthermore, the spatial carrier pattern is conditioned on the semantic segmentation mask of the image. For example, high-frequency textural regions (foliage, animal fur, asphalt) receive higher watermark amplitude, while smooth gradients (blue skies, studio backdrops) receive minimal perturbation. RemoveSynth addresses this multi-scale embedding by applying a pyramidal wavelet decomposition that isolates each octave independently before executing phase decorrelation.
    </p>

    <h2>Google Veo Frame-by-Frame Watermark Neutralization</h2>
    <p>
      Google Veo text-to-video generation expands spatial watermarking into the time domain. If an identical spatial pattern were stamped onto each frame, temporal averaging across 30 consecutive frames would easily expose the static pattern, allowing simple subtraction attacks. To prevent this, Veo modulates the spatial carrier with a pseudorandom temporal drift function synchronized to the video framerate (24 fps or 30 fps).
    </p>
    <p>
      When sanitizing Veo MP4 or WebM video exports, RemoveSynth demuxes the container bitstream into raw compressed frames. Our client-side pipeline applies a forward-backward temporal phase jitter filter across chrominance sub-channels ($Cb$ and $Cr$). By slightly shifting the temporal phase alignment while keeping luminance ($Y$) motion vectors intact, the cross-frame coherence required by Google video watermark detector is broken without causing visual motion judder or dropping frames.
    </p>

    <h2>Google Lyria Audio Spectral Scrubbing</h2>
    <p>
      In audio generation, Google Lyria embeds acoustic watermarks into synthesized waveforms before delivery. The watermark signal takes the form of narrow-band frequency modulations placed beneath the masking thresholds of prominent harmonics. Because human psychoacoustics cannot perceive soft sounds occurring immediately adjacent to loud tones (simultaneous spectral masking), the watermark remains inaudible while surviving 128 kbps MP3 compression and analog room acoustics.
    </p>
    <p>
      RemoveSynth audio engine processes sound files through an adaptive short-time Fourier transform (STFT). The algorithm calculates the spectral envelope and identifies phase-locked harmonic clusters. By applying bounded random phase dithering ($\pm 0.04$ radians) strictly to the masked frequency bins, the artificial periodic signature is dispersed into broadband ambient thermal noise, rendering automated acoustic identification completely ineffective.
    </p>

    <h2>Text Token De-biasing for Gemini and Gemma Models</h2>
    <p>
      SynthID for text operates entirely differently from continuous media watermarks. Rather than modifying an existing file, SynthID text alters the autoregressive decoding process. When Gemini or Gemma predicts the next word, a cryptographic pseudo-random function assigns a score $g(v) \in [0, 1]$ to each token in the vocabulary based on preceding context tokens. Tokens with high $g$-scores receive artificially elevated selection probabilities.
    </p>
    <p>
      Over long passages, the cumulative distribution of $g$-scores reveals a distinct statistical bias that detectors flag with high certainty. To neutralize SynthID in text, RemoveSynth text processor identifies hash anchor points and introduces contextual synonym substitutions. Changing one non-essential word every 10 to 15 tokens completely scrambles the subsequent hash seeds, collapsing the detector statistical confidence back to random baseline levels.
    </p>

